{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=json.loads(Path('tasks/.plans/rate-limit/task-plan.json').read_text())\nprint('Plan keys:', ', '.join(p))\nfor u in p['tasks'] if 'tasks' in p else p['units']:\n    print(json.dumps({k:u.get(k) for k in ['id','depends_on','creates_paths','touches_paths','done_condition']},indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Plan keys: api_version, approved, kind, metadata, units\n{\n  \"id\": \"T-20260802-decision-visibility\",\n  \"depends_on\": [\n    \"T-20260802-request-101\"\n  ],\n  \"creates_paths\": [\n    \"src/rate_limit/decision.py\",\n    \"tests/test_decision_visibility.py\"\n  ],\n  \"touches_paths\": [],\n  \"done_condition\": \"The denial response and telemetry carry the same stable reason and decision identifier.\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"T-20260802-effective-policy\",\n  \"depends_on\": [\n    \"T-20260802-policy-schema\"\n  ],\n  \"creates_paths\": [\n    \"src/rate_limit/policy.py\",\n    \"tests/test_policy_resolution.py\"\n  ],\n  \"touches_paths\": [],\n  \"done_condition\": \"The resolver returns one stable policy and provenance for every tested input.\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"T-20260802-policy-schema\",\n  \"depends_on\": [],\n  \"creates_paths\": [\n    \"src/rate_limit/policy.schema.json\",\n    \"tests/test_policy_schema.py\"\n  ],\n  \"touches_paths\": [],\n  \"done_condition\": \"A JSON Schema accepts valid limits and windows and rejects invalid values.\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"T-20260802-request-101\",\n  \"depends_on\": [\n    \"T-20260802-effective-policy\"\n  ],\n  \"creates_paths\": [\n    \"src/rate_limit/enforcement.py\",\n    \"tests/test_request_101.py\"\n  ],\n  \"touches_paths\": [],\n  \"done_condition\": \"Requests 1-100 are allowed and request 101 is denied for the same organization window.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"The four tasks are separate because each owns **one distinct decision, a bounded write surface, and its own completion checks**. Their outputs form a sequential dependency chain.\n\n### Boundaries\n\nTask names below omit the shared `T-20260802-` prefix.\n\n| Task / owner | Responsibility and boundary | Planned completion evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| **policy-schema** / `platform-contracts` | Turn authored policy into a validated document. Owns policy shape and valid values. | Accept valid limits/windows; reject invalid values. |\n| **effective-policy** / `policy-runtime` | Consume validated policy and organization identity; return one effective policy with provenance. Owns override/default precedence. | Overrides win deterministically; explicit defaults apply when absent; provenance is returned. |\n| **request-101** / `request-runtime` | Consume effective policy, organization identity, and counter state; produce an allow/deny decision. Owns enforcement before forwarding. | Allow requests 1–100 and deny request 101 within the same organization/window. |\n| **decision-visibility** / `reliability-interface` | Consume the enforcement decision; produce the response reason and telemetry. Owns presentation and correlation. | Both outputs carry `organization_rate_limit_exceeded` and the same decision identifier. |\n\nThese boundaries and checks are recorded in the [seam definitions](</private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat tvl5i7ya/tasks/.plans/rate-limit/seams>).\n\nEach task creates a different implementation file—`policy.schema.json`, `policy.py`, `enforcement.py`, or `decision.py`—plus its focused test file. The [compiled task plan](</private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat tvl5i7ya/tasks/.plans/rate-limit/task-plan.json>) declares no overlapping writes.\n\n### Dependencies\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[policy-schema] -->|Validated policy| B[effective-policy]\n  B -->|Effective policy| C[request-101]\n  C -->|Allow/deny decision| D[decision-visibility]\n```\n\nThis is the explicit chain in the [current graph](</private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat tvl5i7ya/tasks/.plans/rate-limit/proposed-graph.json>). **Independently assessable completion does not mean independent inputs:** downstream proof still needs its declared dependencies.\n\nThe recorded reasons for separation are concrete:\n\n- Combining policy work inside the API handler mixes validation, resolution, and enforcement.\n- Repeating resolution in each adapter risks inconsistent precedence.\n- Telemetry observes enforcement; it cannot make or authorize the decision.\n- A metric alone cannot give callers a stable denial reason.\n\nOrganization identity comes from the host application; window/counter state comes from an implementation-selected adapter. Provider metering, production storage selection, and deployment infrastructure remain outside scope. See the [system map](</private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat tvl5i7ya/tasks/.plans/rate-limit/system-map.md>) and [intent](</private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat tvl5i7ya/tasks/.plans/rate-limit/intent.md>).\n\n### Current evidence status\n\nCLI inspection reports **compiled, all four tasks not materialized, 0/4 accepted**. All four capabilities remain unproven, with **“integration proof task missing”** reported for each. The reviewed plan explains the intended separation; it does not establish implemented behavior.\n\nThe CLI’s next lifecycle step is `taskspec batch --plan tasks/.plans/rate-limit/task-plan.json`, which creates unsealed task scaffolds."}}
